WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.835 --> 00:00:07.210
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI:
Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.910
We're very excited
to be here today

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.360
and to talk to you
about unlocking

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.700
genomic workloads on the cloud.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.860
Part of why we decided
to have this talk

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.860
is that the life sciences
industry is actually

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:21.580
undergoing a monumental
shift in the way it works.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.505
And cloud is actually
becoming central

00:00:23.505 --> 00:00:27.020
in the way they work
and conduct research.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:30.970
This talk is specifically
focused around genomics

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.610
and how technology is
completely changing

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:37.670
fields like diagnostics and the
development of new treatments

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.410
with the use of data.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.340
We'll start by providing an
overview of the landscape today

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.560
and where we are.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.310
Then, we're going to
be talking about some

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.920
of the challenges with genomics.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.470
And we're going to be
talking about how cloud can

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.000
help solve these challenges.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.750
And then we're going to be
going through some tooling

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.200
and how to actually use some of
this genomic specific tooling

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.402
on GCP.

00:01:00.402 --> 00:01:02.110
And finally, we're
going to be showcasing

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.170
a demo of how to do that.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.560
So my name is
Hussain Al Muscati.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.370
I work as a customer engineer in
Google Cloud, primarily focused

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.003
on public sector
and life sciences.

00:01:14.003 --> 00:01:15.670
HATEM NAWAR: And my
name is Hatem Nawar.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.450
I also work as a customer
engineer in Google Cloud,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.102
focused on life sciences.

00:01:21.102 --> 00:01:23.060
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: We
are both based in London

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:27.050
and are presenting to you
today from our humble homes.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:30.650
As customer engineers, we work
very closely with our customers

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:34.130
to help enable them
to adopt and use Cloud

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.360
and to help them develop
solutions that are Cloud-based.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.260
And our primary focus is
working with public institutions

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.870
and research organizations.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:49.150
So to kick off, there is
an interesting statement

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:54.160
that says there'll be 163
zettabytes of data by 2025.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.860
I'll give you a few seconds for
you to actually absorb that.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.660
So they give you some context.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:12.850
That is equivalent to watching
a cat video that is about twice

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.070
as long as the universe is old.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.440
And to give you another
way of looking at it,

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.820
all the data that has been
generated by mankind up

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:26.650
to the 1980s is less
than a zettabyte.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.810
So that means a majority of
this data is actually not new.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:31.990
So where's all this
data coming from?

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:36.220
Well, I mean, part of it is from
you taking pictures, uploading

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.500
them, videos, et cetera.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.080
But a big part of it is actually
from health care and life

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.220
sciences.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.070
Medical imaging is
actually number one.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.990
But genomics is not
that far from it.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.980
And the amount of genomics
data is actually exponentially

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.860
growing and resulting
in a huge challenge

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:57.280
in terms of how we can handle
it and manage this data.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.960
This growth means
that there needs

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.130
to be like a new medium
or way of handling it.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.060
And what is the
end result of this?

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:10.700
Well, we're already seeing a
profound impact on health care.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.500
We've barely
scratched the surface

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:15.720
of what genomic research
can actually yield.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.560
But it's already having
a significant impact.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.540
In 2018, around 1/3 of
new FDA-approved drugs

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.830
actually relied on genomics.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:30.180
And interesting enough,
in 2005, Francis Collins,

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.320
the director of the National
Human Genome Research

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.880
Institute, predicted
that most drugs by 2020

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:41.630
would be derived or used
using the understanding

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:43.200
of the human genome.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.440
And we're actually
not that far from it.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.820
And another interesting fact is
by this year, the expectation,

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.250
in 2020, the expectation
that there'll

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:56.930
be around 26 million people who
have shared their DNA with one

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.660
of the four leading
commercial ancestry forms.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.160
So this is actually
becoming a trend.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.940
Another interesting
trend is what we call

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.970
the rise of genomic biobanks.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.290
It's quite interesting.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:14.120
Biobanking is the result of
advances in human genome,

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.970
in computing, and
bioinformatics.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:18.920
And if you think
of it, it's really

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:22.290
a modern way of
storing, managing,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.390
and analyzing massive
amounts of biological data.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:29.270
Many population-based
biobanks have been

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.430
formed in different countries.

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.730
And it's actually becoming
essential national strategy.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.610
Some examples include
the UK Biobank,

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.700
the FinnGen Project in
Finland, and the Biobank Graz

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.260
in Austria.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.380
And biobanking is
actually quite important

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:50.240
because they act as a central
hub for other organizations,

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.990
such as farmers, universities,
research institutes.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:58.280
And they provide a way for these
institutes or organizations

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:01.060
to access data to
conduct research.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.170
They are strategic,
very strategic,

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:06.800
because they help the
industry overall and form

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.030
as an accelerator for research.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:13.988
Yet, they still face
very common challenges.

00:05:13.988 --> 00:05:15.280
So let's talk a bit about that.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:22.930
Because sequencing has become a
lot more prevalent and cheaper,

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:26.890
we're generally getting a
huge increase in genomic data.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:33.682
And this rapid growth is
really in petabytes of data.

00:05:33.682 --> 00:05:35.140
And that means when
you're actually

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.780
needing to share that data
among other organizations,

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.020
you're talking about an
exobyte level of sharing.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:44.800
This large growth of data,
together with the complexity

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.410
of having to analyze
a lot of this data,

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.530
requires a new scale
of compute and storage.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:52.030
And when we talking
new scale, we're

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:55.150
not talking a few
hundred or thousands VMs.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.940
Like in many cases
that we've seen,

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.160
you'd need something
like 100,000 cores

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.750
to be able to do one process
from a result of a sequencing

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:06.130
activity.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:10.960
Most organizations don't
have that scale or resource.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.360
And in many cases,
they need to be

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.310
able to do it in
adversity way, such

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:18.230
that they suddenly
receive a large data dump

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:21.790
and they need to process it in
a very short period of time.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.610
What's happening because of this
is that technology is actually

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.390
becoming the limiter of research
because many organizations are

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:33.100
not set up to be able to
handle this level of compute.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:36.770
So how does Cloud actually
help solve this problem?

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:40.510
Well, if you start by looking
at the traditional way

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:43.660
in which things worked and
when you say traditional,

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:47.590
we mean this is how biobanks,
research institutes,

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:51.860
and pharmas all are
set up to work today.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.880
This is the environment
and process that they have

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.730
when the volumes
of data are small

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:01.060
and the requirements were
actually quite different.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.830
And how that works is that
all the data is centralized

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.670
and in a repository.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.730
And whenever any
researcher or organization

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:12.340
wants to conduct
research on that data,

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:14.770
they basically make
a copy and use it

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:17.140
on their own
workstation or computer

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:21.512
cluster, where effectively
data sharing meant data copy.

00:07:21.512 --> 00:07:23.470
So if you think about
it, how is this practical

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.770
when you have petabytes of data?

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:29.260
How do you actually manage
to process all that data

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.630
if you're talking about
petabytes of data?

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:33.940
This requires
considerable compute.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:36.640
And many of these
organizations have what

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:39.310
effectively is fixed compute.

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:41.740
And they only hardware.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:47.080
And they don't have the ability
to scale 200,000 cores to be

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:49.080
able to process that data.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:52.720
And with many scientists, they
would have to wait in a queue

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:55.690
because a specific
organization would only

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:57.580
have one compute cluster.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.670
And for different sciences
to be able to conduct work,

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:03.550
they have to wait until
the other ones are done.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:05.650
And another challenge
that this actually poses

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:09.460
is security implementation,
because the way that data

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:14.500
is shared, each time it's shared
in a different way and copied,

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:18.220
that means you need to put
specific custom security

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:21.040
control mechanism
for each activity,

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:22.860
for each sharing of data.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:25.300
And that actually makes
it quite vulnerable

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:28.060
because you don't have a
comprehensive overarching

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:30.190
security methodology.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:33.679
So how does public Cloud
actually change this model?

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:37.840
Well, in public Cloud, all
the data and the compute

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:39.039
would be centralized.

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:41.470
And the researchers
and the institutions

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:43.750
would go to that
data and compute.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:47.530
So there would only be one
copy of the data maintained.

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:51.580
And how we actually
have access to that data

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:53.050
would be controlled
as using things

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:57.220
like IAM, Identity
Access Management, where

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:01.330
each organization or individual
that needs to access that data

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:02.170
is controlled.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:05.110
The access is controlled
at a very granular level

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:06.920
such that the only
access to the data

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.930
they need, and only perform
the actions they need to

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:13.130
for the benefit of the research.

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:15.880
And at the same time,
because it's cloud-based,

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.160
it provides an elastic level
of compute and storage,

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:22.120
such that if they
suddenly need to do

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.850
high-level processing
with 100,000 cores,

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:26.200
they have access to that.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.330
And it's elastic in
the sense that they

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.840
don't need to preserve
that or reserve

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:35.590
that compute power beforehand.

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:37.120
They use it when they need it.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:39.420
And they only use it
while they need it.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.600
And this also gives a benefit
of having a centralized security

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:47.770
implementation such that the
way to control security here

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:51.130
is to provide access to
individuals or organizations

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:52.760
in a very controlled way.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:55.390
And you would have a
comprehensive security

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.250
method implemented.

00:09:57.250 --> 00:10:01.240
So we talked quite a bit about
some of the tooling that's

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:06.040
available and in terms of
like what Cloud can provide

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.830
in terms of high level
compute and storage

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:10.150
and the benefits of that.

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:13.150
Next, we're going to be talking
about specific tooling that

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.850
is custom made for
genomic data that

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:20.560
can be either deployed or
natively available on GCP.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:22.120
Onto you, Hatem,
to talk about that.

00:10:24.857 --> 00:10:26.190
HATEM NAWAR: Thank you, Hussain.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.370
There are many tools that are
available to bioinformatician's

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:32.440
trying to use GCP for
genomics analysis.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:35.630
So let's have a look
at some of these tools.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:38.720
Hail is one of the common tools
that provide powerful data

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:42.080
science tools that can be used
to interrogate large scale

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:43.240
data.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:45.380
Hail uses Spark in
Hadoop, which means

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.080
it can be deployed on
top of Dataproc, which

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:50.360
is our managed Hadoop service.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:53.270
And Hail actually has
native support for Dataproc.

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:55.960
And we will see
more of this later.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:59.530
Pipelines on top of
workflow management engines

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:03.310
are also among the common tools
many bioinformaticians use.

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:05.530
We have built the
life sciences API

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:07.660
to make it easy for
these workflow engines

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:09.760
to integrate with
Google Cloud and be

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:12.010
able to run at cloud scale.

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:13.690
Some examples of
the workflow engines

00:11:13.690 --> 00:11:16.690
that take advantage of
the life sciences API

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:19.690
are Cromwell, which is
developed by the Road Institute.

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:22.270
Nextflow and Snakemake are
two other workflow engines

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:26.430
that are open source and are
very popular in the community.

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:28.100
These workflow
engines are commonly

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:30.950
used by biobanks and
research organization

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:34.160
to process and analyze thousands
of whole genome sequences

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:34.670
with ease.

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:40.500
But working with common
file formats for genomics

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.660
and variant data
can be challenging

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:46.260
when working with machine
learning frameworks, which

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:48.180
was one of the
drivers of developing

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:50.220
the variants transform tool.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.530
The variants transform
tool is an open source tool

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:55.770
developed by Google Cloud
and now has many contributors

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:57.370
from the community.

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:00.150
It can export variants
data to BigQuery.

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:01.680
This makes it easy
to interrogate

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:06.210
the data using SQL statements in
a very fast and efficient way.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:09.210
It also makes it quite easy
to build data sets that

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.340
can be consumed easily
by machine learning

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:14.880
frameworks or our AI platform
to build new machine learning

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.080
models.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:18.850
But sometimes, all
the researchers

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:22.090
want is to simply access
raw compute power.

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:23.590
It could be that
they need a machine

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:26.620
with more than 100 cores
or even terabytes of memory

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:29.530
to run something like a
de novo gene assembly,

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:32.230
or use an HPC
cluster such as slurm

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:34.030
or any number of
the tools that they

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:38.022
can run on top of Google Compute
Engine such as Galaxy or Toil,

00:12:38.022 --> 00:12:39.730
which can actually
use the Compute Engine

00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:43.917
APIs to provision instances.

00:12:43.917 --> 00:12:45.500
Many of the
bioinformaticians may also

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:49.160
be using research and analysis
platform such as Tera, which

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:52.520
was developed by Verily, which
is an Alphabet company, also

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:54.980
by the Broad Institute
to enable researchers

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:58.730
to collaborate on
workspaces, share notebooks,

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:01.950
and easily launch pipelines
through Cromwell Integrations.

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:05.060
This makes such analysis
platforms a one-stop shop

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:08.060
to access data sets and be
able to run your analysis

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:11.833
and share that and
collaborate with others.

00:13:11.833 --> 00:13:13.500
And of course, there
are many more tools

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:16.410
from the community and
our partner ecosystems

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:22.150
that can be added to this tool
box for bioinformaticians.

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:25.240
So let us look at Hail
in a more bit of details.

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:28.360
As I mentioned before, Hail
runs on top of Dataproc.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:31.720
And Dataproc enables you not
just to create Hadoop cluster

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:34.810
in seconds, but also
enables you to easily scale

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:36.760
up and down this cluster.

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:39.970
This flexibility is one of
the key advantages of running

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:41.680
Hail on Google Cloud.

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:43.480
You can easily create
a small cluster

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:45.430
with just a couple
of worker nodes

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:48.610
to configure, debug,
and test your code.

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:52.420
And once you're ready to run
your large [INAUDIBLE] analysis

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:54.550
or large variant
calling analysis,

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:56.470
you can easily
scale up to hundreds

00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:58.150
or even thousands of nodes.

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.760
And you can also add
preemptable instances,

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:02.890
which can help speed
up execution and keep

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:05.570
the costs down.

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:07.750
Once you're done, you
can scale down or simply

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:10.870
delete the cluster, as it only
takes you a couple of minutes

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:12.910
to provision really a new one.

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:15.610
There is no more waiting in a
queue for the local cluster,

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:19.180
or being limited by
the on prem capacity.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:23.030
The Dataproc integration is now
part of the core Hail library.

00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:25.280
You can create a Hail
cluster from the Cloud

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.010
Shell in just a couple of
minutes with just these two

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:29.820
lines you see on the screen.

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:31.580
It is insanely easy.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.980
If you have attempted to
deploy a Hadoop or Spark

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:35.780
cluster before and
configure and install

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:37.730
software in its
workers, you would

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:39.390
know that it can take days.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.630
And you may still not
be able to achieve

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:43.190
the performance
and scale you would

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:44.390
get from running on Cloud.

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:51.020
Many of the existing tools
use common genomics format,

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:55.320
such as fast queue, [? bams, ?]
[? crams, ?] VCFs, et cetera.

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.350
But building data sets
out of these file formats

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:01.590
or cross examining with other
data coming from health care

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:04.800
systems, imaging, or even
[INAUDIBLE] or weather data

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:07.500
can be really challenging.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:09.920
The variants transform
is an open source tool

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:13.670
that uses data flow to export
your variant data to BigQuery

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:15.520
on a standard schema.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:18.180
The use of data flow
allows you to scale

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:21.410
if you have a large data set,
as this could otherwise be

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:24.450
a very time consuming process.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:26.800
BigQuery requires
zero management

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:28.960
and allows you to store
and query petabytes

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:31.810
of data in minutes,
sometimes even seconds

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:34.750
without the hassle of
managing any infrastructure

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:38.360
and only paying for the
queries you execute.

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:40.160
There is already lots
of public genomics

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:42.830
data sets that you can
explore today on the query,

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:46.220
such as the 1,000
genome, platinum genomes,

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:50.810
or the public access
portion of the TCGA.

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:53.270
There is also multiple
non-human variant data,

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:55.220
such as the genomics
cannabis, or the rice

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:59.660
3K, which has variants that
were called using the variants.

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:02.210
The other key advantage to
having the data in BigQuery

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:04.370
is how easy it is
to create cohorts

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:07.670
and data sets that can be used
in Tensorflow or other machine

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:09.480
learning frameworks easily.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:12.830
And you can take advantage
of the AI platform tools

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:16.160
such as [INAUDIBLE] notebooks to
interact with your variant data

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:20.220
using Python or R very easily.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:23.360
So Color is one of our
customers who did just that.

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:25.100
There is a very
interesting blog post

00:16:25.100 --> 00:16:28.520
on our website on how they use
variance transform and BigQuery

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.410
to create machine
learning models that

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:33.220
beat standard industry
models in just weeks.

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:38.160
So one of the reasons
many have chosen

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.530
Google Cloud for the
genomics workloads

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:43.770
is that we don't just
provide infrastructure.

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:45.450
Our product and
engineering teams

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:48.120
have developed a number
of services and APIs

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:50.760
that cater to the needs of
health care and life sciences

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:54.210
community, such as the
health care API that supports

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:57.270
health records in fire
and [INAUDIBLE] format,

00:16:57.270 --> 00:17:01.760
or the imaging API to
interact with DICOM.

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:03.580
The life sciences
API was developed

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:06.400
to enable workflow
engines to take advantage

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:09.650
of Google Cloud in large scale.

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:12.410
This is a reference architecture
for running workflows

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:13.950
at scale and GCP.

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:16.460
Raw data from sequencers
or other sources

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:19.460
typically would be uploaded
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:21.170
And the workflow
engine were drawn

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:23.810
on a Google Compute Engine,
or in your own infrastructure

00:17:23.810 --> 00:17:25.140
or machine.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:28.780
And that would make REST calls
to the life sciences API.

00:17:28.780 --> 00:17:32.090
The life sciences API takes
care of the full lifecycle

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:35.240
of each step in the pipeline,
from creating a virtual machine

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:38.170
with exactly the number of
cores and memory specified,

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:41.240
localizing the input files
and copying them from storage,

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:43.830
pulling Docker images,
and running that step,

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:47.000
then delocalizing the output
again to the cloud storage.

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.640
And it is able to
do that in scale.

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:51.140
The Broad Institute,
for example,

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:54.440
have used the API to call
variants for all 79,000

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:56.740
whole genome
sequences in the Nomad

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:58.400
data set in just three days.

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:04.720
Several workflow engines or
batch job managers like dsub

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:07.270
take advantage of the
life sciences API.

00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:09.140
This includes Cromwell,
as we said before,

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:10.810
which was developed
by the Broad,

00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:13.780
Nextflow, which we will
see later in the demo,

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:16.690
and Snakemake, which
is a recent addition.

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:20.560
All these workflow engines
have very active user community

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:23.140
and with many standard and
sample pipelines in [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:25.520
that you can easily run.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:29.030
The life sciences API makes
it easy for the developers

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:31.040
of these workflow
engines to build quickly

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:33.500
on top of Google
Cloud and also include

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:36.200
tools that allows you
to debug your pipeline

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:39.770
or generate data that can be
used to analyze your pipeline,

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:42.380
calculate the compute
costs of the pipeline,

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:45.980
or a particular step
in the pipeline.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:49.333
On the right-hand side, this
is what a typical pipeline

00:18:49.333 --> 00:18:50.000
might look like.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:51.860
It's a previous
series of tasks that

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:54.830
may include scattering
across multiple processes,

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:56.660
or gathering and collating them.

00:18:56.660 --> 00:19:00.330
For each step, the API will
create a virtual machine.

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:04.070
Once the step is completed,
the virtual machine is deleted.

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:06.320
This helps keep the
cost of the pipeline

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:09.110
down, as you don't have
to pay for a cluster

00:19:09.110 --> 00:19:10.280
and standing all the time.

00:19:10.280 --> 00:19:14.710
And you don't have to pay
for any idle instances.

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:18.760
Now let us run a sample pipeline
on Google Cloud using Nextflow.

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:21.070
This is a sample
configuration for a profile

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:24.400
to run with Google
life sciences executor.

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:26.650
You'll notice I have
specified the region I

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:29.290
want my compute instances
to be and the bucket

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:31.960
used for temporary and
intermediate files.

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:34.480
I've also selected which
region the life sciences

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:36.880
API will store the metadata
about the execution

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:38.230
of the pipeline.

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:41.590
And finally, I said they use
private addresses to true

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:44.080
as they already have a NAD
gateway set up in the project.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:47.980
And I don't want to use public
IP's for added security.

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:50.150
Now, let's switch to
my virtual machine.

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:54.440
So now I'm going to
run a test pipeline

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.380
I wrote on my virtual machine.

00:19:57.380 --> 00:19:59.180
To install and run
Nextflow, I need

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:01.160
to set some
environmental variables.

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:05.270
I've already done that in my
bashrc to save time every time

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:06.330
I'm using this.

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:08.930
So as you can see,
I've added the key

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:11.090
to my service account,
the version I want to run,

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:14.150
and Google as an execution mode.

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:16.720
So now I'm going to
run the pipeline.

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:18.860
I'm going to paste in
a config file, which

00:20:18.860 --> 00:20:23.220
has all my configurations
and project information.

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:26.690
And I'm going to run
my test pipeline, which

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:30.410
just compares the performance
of GPU versus CPU.

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:35.360
I'm going to select the
Google life sciences profile.

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:37.880
And I'm going to add
some options to actually

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:43.672
be able to debug and monitor
the performance of my pipeline.

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:54.920
So as you can see, the
pipeline is now running.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:56.860
It will automatically
create a virtual machine

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:58.960
on Google Cloud.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:02.870
To see that on tower, I can
see that now my workflow

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:04.423
is running.

00:21:04.423 --> 00:21:06.590
I can check on the resources
and the virtual machine

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:08.540
that it's using.

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:13.460
And I can actually monitor
what is being executed.

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:16.940
Spoiler alert, the GPU
beats the CPU in this one.

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:20.660
I can also check previous
pipelines that I've ran before.

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:22.530
With tower, I can
check the CPU usage.

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:25.160
So I can actually
optimize my pipeline

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:27.380
and select the correct
virtual machine shape.

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:31.070
I can check memory,
execution time, and so on.

00:21:31.070 --> 00:21:33.590
It's actually very helpful
when troubleshooting

00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:37.620
and actually optimizing
your pipelines for Cloud.

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:38.760
So that was our demo.

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:41.340
I hope you enjoyed
it as much as I did.

